shtest
======

A simple test runner and assertion library for shell scripts and small
command-line utitlities.

Usage
-----

	shtest (<file>|<dir>) [(<file>|<dir>)...]

Getting Started
---------------

Run the `configure` script to generate an executable which can
then be placed/symlinked anywhere in your PATH.

After the executable has been generated, run the tests as shown below.

	$ bin/shtest tests/
	tests/test_assert_equal.sh

	test_fails_if_strings_are_not_equal...ok
	test_succeeds_if_strings_are_equal...ok
	test_prints_message_if_strings_are_not_equal...ok
	test_does_not_print_message_if_strings_are_equal...ok

	tests/test_assert_exit_code.sh

	test_prints_message_on_failure...ok
	test_does_not_print_message_on_success...ok
	test_fails_if_incorrect_exit_code...ok
	test_succeeds_if_correct_exit_code...ok
	test_executes_command_in_isolation...ok
	test_does_not_throw_error_if_command_contains_whitespace...ok
	test_does_not_throw_error_if_command_contains_quotes...ok

	tests/test_shtest.sh

	test_extracts_test_names_from_a_file_and_runs_them...ok
	test_runs_all_tests_in_each_test_file_specified...ok
	test_runs_each_test_file_in_isolation...ok
	test_ignores_functions_which_do_not_start_with_test_...ok
	test_runs_each_test_function_in_isolation...ok
	test_also_extracts_function_names_with_different_valid_formatting...ok
	test_only_extracts_names_of_defined_functions...ok
	test_assertions_are_available_in_tests...ok
	test_stderr_received_during_test_execution_displayed_after_result...ok
	test_recurses_into_directories_passed_as_arguments...ok


Writing Tests
-------------

`shtest` will run any function in the given file which begins with `test_`. If
the test function returns 0 then the test passes, otherwise it fails.

You can use the following assertions in the tests, which wrap the `test`
utility to add helpful error messages to make debugging failures easier.

	# shtest/assert.sh

	assert_equal () {
		# $1, $2 - strings to compare
		if test "$1" != "$2"
		then
			printf "expected: '%s'\nactual: '%s'\n\n" "$1" "$2"
			return 1
		fi
	}

	assert_exit_code () {
		# $1 - expected exit code
		# $2 - command to be executed
		( eval "$2" )

		set -- "$@" $?  # $3 - actual exit code of command

		if test "$3" -ne "$1"
		then
			printf "expected: '%s'\nactual: '%s'\n" "$1" "$3"
			return 1
		fi
	}

For example:

	assert_equal 'hello' 'world'

Would give the error message:

	expected: 'hello'
	actual: 'world'

Make sure the expected value is the first parameter, and the actual value is
the second.

If a test fails any stderr output captured during the test execution will be
printed to the terminal to help locate the problem. Using the provided
assertion functions should be useful.

	$ shtest test_blah.sh
	test_passes...ok
	test_fails...FAILED
	expected: 'true'
	actual: 'false'

The tests in this repository are written to be run using `shtest`, so the code
tests itself. Read the test code for more usage examples.

Portability
-----------

`shtest` attempts to be as portable as possible. It works on Linux and probably
on macos and BSD, but the tests use `mktemp` which may cause portability
problems. Please raise an issue if you encounter a problem so it can be fixed.
